\subsection{Ablations and additional diagnostics}
While the default results above focus on a single seed (for traceability with the included artifacts), our reference implementation supports:
\begin{itemize}
\item multiple seeds and 95\% CIs for coverage, set size, and compute;
\item ablations over $\alpha\in\{0.05,0.1,0.2\}$ and $K_{\max}\in\{8,16,32\}$;
\item additional baselines: (i) \emph{$q$-consensus}, (ii) fixed-threshold early stopping without conformalization, (iii) ACSC-UB with $m\in\{1,2\}$, and (iv) Hoeffding-based leader-gap stopping.
\end{itemize}
We report p-values for a one-sample binomial z-test comparing the observed naive-ACSC coverage against the target $1-\alpha$ at $n_{\mathrm{test}}=1200$; for the default configuration, $p<10^{-16}$, confirming statistically significant undercoverage for the naive heuristic. The code further outputs effect sizes (Cohen’s $h$) and calibration metrics (ECE/Brier). 

\paragraph{Compute adaptivity.}
On easy instances, vote mass concentrates rapidly and the ACSC-UB superset shrinks quickly, enabling early stopping. On hard ones, many labels remain viable and the procedure can use the full budget. The stopping parameter $m$ controls the aggressiveness of early stopping.

\section{System Design and Implementation}
ACSC is implemented as a thin wrapper around any sampler interface exposing i.i.d. draws conditioned on $x$. The core operations are prefix vote counting, superset pruning via $\text{share\_ub}_t$, and a calibrated threshold $\tau$ obtained once per deployment from a small calibration split. The implementation is model-agnostic and can be integrated into LLM decoding stacks alongside parallel decoding or speculative decoding. Our package provides:
\begin{itemize}
\item a reproducible simulator with command-line interface,
\item multiple seeds aggregation and CI computation,
\item calibration diagnostics (histograms of $v_{K_{\max}}(y^\star)$, reported $\tau$),
\item coverage, set size, and compute logs per instance and in aggregate, and
\item optional ECE/Brier computation using proxy probabilities (e.g., top share at stopping).
\end{itemize}
We ensure deterministic seeding for the dataset and for the solver to facilitate replication and ablation.

\section{Discussion and Limitations}
ACSC provides a knob ($\alpha$) to trade coverage and efficiency, requires only black-box sampling,
and needs a small calibration split. The guarantees are marginal and assume exchangeability between calibration and test; domain shift can degrade coverage \cite{angelopoulos2021gentle}. The naive ACSC rule (prefix thresholding) can under-cover---as demonstrated---and should be avoided if coverage is mandatory. ACSC-UB addresses this by returning a superset of the final conformal set, at the cost of potentially larger sets on hard inputs; the stop-size parameter $m$ trades set size for compute.

We emphasize three limitations and future directions:
(1) Real-LLM evaluation: While we use a controlled simulator for transparency and reproducibility, evaluating ACSC-UB on GSM8K, MATH, and BIG-Bench will provide external validity and stress-test exchangeability. 
(2) Nonconformity design: Our vote-share score can be combined with token likelihoods or rationale consistency for richer scores \cite{angelopoulos2020uncertainty, angelopoulos2022conformal}, potentially shrinking sets.
(3) Conditional guarantees and covariate shift: Mondrian and covariate-shift-aware conformalization can yield finer-grained guarantees \cite{vovk2005algorithmic, angelopoulos2021gentle}. Robustness against prompt or distribution shifts is an open challenge.

\section{Conclusion}
We presented ACSC, a training-free, compute-adaptive decoding method for LLMs. We identified a coverage pitfall in a naive anytime rule and proposed ACSC-UB, a safe superset variant with provable coverage at any stopping time. The method is simple, model-agnostic, and complementary to fast decoding. Our simulator and reference implementation support multiple seeds, ablations, and diagnostics to facilitate reproducibility and future real-LLM evaluation.

\paragraph{Reproducibility.}
We provide a reference implementation and scripts to reproduce all reported numbers and figures, including calibration diagnostics, confidence intervals, ablations over $\alpha$ and $K_{\max}$, and baseline comparisons. All illustrative figures in this draft are generated with PGFPlots for self-contained LaTeX builds; aggregate plots with error bars can be saved directly by the simulator for convenience.

